{#- Home. Built from `content/` alone; every number below is measured, none is
    written into the template. The mockup's own copy quotes a journal count and
    a publication range, and both are wrong now — which is exactly why the
    chrome reads them from the archive instead. The figures themselves are not
    repeated here, so that a grep for them across the templates stays a useful
    check rather than one this comment defeats. -#}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Urban Currents</title>
{{ head_extras }}
<style>
{{ css }}
</style>
</head>
<body>
<main class="uc-page">
  {#- One list, from `site.nav_links` — the issue pages inject the same one
      (0Z, Z4). Two renderers is deliberate; two link lists was drift. -#}
  <nav class="uc-nav">
    {%- for link in nav %}
    <a{% if link.cls %} class="{{ link.cls }}"{% endif %} href="{{ link.href }}">{{ link.label }}</a>
    {%- endfor %}
    <span class="uc-nav__lang">EN</span>
  </nav>

  <header class="uc-mission">
    <h1 class="uc-mission__title">
      A daily scan of urban data science: what appeared, what it found, and why
      it matters.
    </h1>
    <p class="uc-mission__lede">
      {{ stats.arxiv_categories }} arXiv categories, {{ stats.journals }} journals —
      {% if stats.range_low == stats.range_high %}
      about {{ stats.range_low }} papers a day worth your time.
      {% else %}
      the {{ stats.range_low }}–{{ stats.range_high }} papers a day worth your time.
      {% endif %}
      When the signal is weak, we say so. Free, no ads, no engagement metrics.
    </p>
    {#- No subscription form. There is no domain, no sending infrastructure and
        no list, and a form that does nothing is a lie told in HTML. Phase 1
        fills this in when there is something behind it. -#}
  </header>

  {% if latest %}
  {#- Two columns, per mockup 5a: the lead on the left and a 210px stat rail on
      the right with a rule between them. It was one column, which is most of
      why the page did not look like the mockup. Flex rather than grid so 7b's
      mobile behaviour — the rail dropping below the lead as a row — is one
      `flex-direction` change. -#}
  <section class="uc-latest">
    <div class="uc-latest__head">
      <h2 class="uc-latest__title">Latest issue — {{ latest.date }}</h2>
      <a class="uc-latest__read" href="issues/{{ latest.date }}.html">Read →</a>
    </div>
    <div class="uc-latest__cols">
      <div class="uc-latest__lede">
        {#- The headline line whole. It is a full sentence and there is room for
            it; cutting it mid-clause in the template would be rewriting the
            pipeline's output at render time. -#}
        <p class="uc-latest__lead">{{ lead_line }}</p>
        {% if synthesis and synthesis.paragraph %}
        <p class="uc-latest__note">{{ synthesis.paragraph }}</p>
        {% endif %}
      </div>
      <dl class="uc-latest__stats">
        <div><dt>items</dt><dd>{{ latest.scan_meta.items_published }}</dd></div>
        <div><dt>candidates scanned</dt><dd>{{ latest.scan_meta.candidates_scanned }}</dd></div>
        <div><dt>with code / data</dt><dd class="uc-latest__accent">{{ latest_code }} / {{ latest_data }}</dd></div>
        <div><dt>no open abstract</dt><dd class="uc-latest__quiet">{{ latest.scan_meta.unreadable_count }}</dd></div>
        {#- Three states, not two. A day with no shift and a day whose baseline
            was too thin to compare against are different facts, and printing
            `0` for the second is the mistake this project keeps auditing out
            of itself. -#}
        <div><dt>tag shift</dt><dd>{{ tag_shift }}</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>
  {% endif %}

  <section class="uc-archive">
    <div class="uc-archive__head">
      <h2 class="uc-archive__title">Archive — last {{ rows|length }} days</h2>
      <a class="uc-archive__all" href="archive.html">All issues →</a>
    </div>
    {#- Mockup 5a's 14-bar histogram. Height is proportional to what was
        published; the colour says which kind of day it was.

        **The colour is never the only carrier.** Every bar has a `title` and an
        `aria-label` naming the day, its count and its kind, so a reader who
        cannot see the accent — or is reading with a screen reader — gets the
        same three facts. That is 0j's verification 7, and it is why the caption
        under the strip spells out what grey means in words. -#}
    <div class="uc-spark" role="img"
         aria-label="Published items per day over the last {{ rows|length }} days.">
      {% for bar in spark %}
      <div class="uc-spark__slot{% if bar.recent %} uc-spark__slot--recent{% endif %}">
        <div class="uc-spark__bar uc-spark__bar--{{ bar.kind }}"
             style="height: {{ bar.height }}%"
             title="{{ bar.label }}"></div>
        <span class="uc-visually-hidden">{{ bar.label }}</span>
      </div>
      {% endfor %}
    </div>
    {#- Every state the strip can draw has a name here (0Z, Z8).
        `base.css.j2` states the rule the old caption only half kept: colour
        never carries the meaning on its own, so a state that is drawn is a
        state that is named. -#}
    <p class="uc-spark__caption">
      <span>{{ spark_from }}</span>
      <span class="uc-spark__legend">
        grey — a quiet day, nothing published ·
        hatched — not seen ·
        dashed — filled in later ·
        underlined — still filling in
      </span>
      <span>{{ spark_to }}</span>
    </p>
    {% with issue_href = "issues/" %}{% include "_archive_rows.html.j2" %}{% endwith %}
  </section>

  {#- How this is made (Launch A, A2). No separate About page — YJUN's call —
      and this is the page somebody arriving from a search result lands on, so
      the answer has to be here.

      **The unflattering number is in the middle of it, not at the end.** If a
      reader is going to find out that the filter misses the bar we set, they
      should find out from us. The vocabulary is not repeated here: the legend
      under the archive strip above already defines every mark, and saying it
      twice on one page is the noise 0Z-C removed.

      Every figure is passed in. `quality` is measured from the labels; when it
      is absent the sentence is left out rather than printed with a zero. -#}
  <section class="uc-method">
    <h2 class="uc-method__h2">How this is made</h2>
    {#- ★ "Every weekday morning" was wrong and had been since the schedule was
        turned on (0Z-F, S5). `daily.yml`'s only cron is `0 21 * * *` — every
        day, weekends included, and the archive shows Saturday and Sunday
        issues. It is the kind of error that survives because it sounds like
        the shape of the thing rather than a claim about it. -#}
    <p class="uc-method__p">
      Every morning, seven days a week, an unattended pipeline reads
      {{ stats.arxiv_categories }} arXiv categories and {{ stats.journals }}
      journals. A journal article enters because a journal we track published
      it. An arXiv preprint enters on a classifier's probability, above a floor
      set from labelled examples. Neither path is asked to fill a quota, which
      is why some days are short and some are empty.
    </p>
    {#- What the filter is *for*, which the page had never said (0Z-F, S5).
        Deliberately short on provenance: who labelled and to what standard is
        not written down anywhere this template can read, so the claim stops at
        what is checkable — the labels are ours, they were applied by hand, and
        the classifier extends a human judgement rather than replacing it.

        "computational urban science" is lower-cased on purpose. *Computational
        Urban Science* is also one of the journals in `vocab/journals.yaml`, and
        the sentence means the field. -#}
    <p class="uc-method__p">
      <strong>What we are looking for:</strong> work that matters to urban AI
      and computational urban science, as the Institute for Latent Publics
      judges it. The examples the floor is set from were labelled here, by
      hand, paper by paper. The classifier is a way of applying that judgement
      to more papers than a person can read — it does not form one of its own.
    </p>
    {%- if quality %}
    <p class="uc-method__p">
      {#- ★ This said "<rate> were worth publishing", which cannot be true of
          papers — there is no fraction of a paper — and it said it in the one
          paragraph where the number *is* the claim.

          The tempting repair is worse. "N of the ten" reads more confident and
          states an integer for a single day, while the figure is `precision@10`
          **averaged over each labelled day**, and the days behind it are not
          alike. So the sentence says `precision was <rate>`, which is what was
          measured.

          The figures are deliberately not spelled out in this comment. The
          header at the top of this file explains why, and
          `test_the_precision_figures_are_not_written_into_the_template` is the
          check a quoted figure here would defeat — which is exactly the shape
          of the accident 0Z-D was: a comment describing a thing, quoting it,
          and breaking the mechanism that watched it. -#}
      <strong>How well that works, measured:</strong> taking the ten papers
      each path ranked highest on a day,
      {% for name, q in quality.sources.items() -%}
      {% if loop.first %}precision was {% endif %}{{ '%.2f'|format(q.precision) }} on the
      {{ q.label }} path{% if not loop.last %}, and {% endif %}
      {%- endfor %}.
      <strong>We set ourselves {{ '%.2f'|format(quality.target) }} and both
      miss it.</strong> Precision holds at the target through about rank
      {{ quality.sources.values()|map(attribute='depth_holding_target')|min }} and
      falls after — so the top of a day is better than these figures, and the
      tail is worse.
      {# ★ The populations came off in 0Z-F (S5), YJUN's call. What replaces
         them is not nothing: a rate quoted bare reads as if it were solid, and
         this one is a mean over a handful of hand-labelled days. The sentence
         that says so has to stay even though the numbers behind it do not.

         Written with `{#`, not `{#-`: the stripping form ate the space between
         two sentences and produced "worse.All of it rests". #}
      All of it rests on a small sample judged by hand, so read these as
      an order of magnitude rather than as figures.
    </p>
    {%- endif %}
    <p class="uc-method__p">
      arXiv indexes about three days late, so the newest rows keep filling in
      after they first appear — in a later issue, never in one already
      published. Bibliography, authors and links come from the sources, never
      from a model; a model writes the summaries and the one-line headline, from
      the abstract and nothing else. Every tag carries a canonical identifier —
      OpenAlex, ORCID, ROR — so a method named twice is one thing, not two.
    </p>
    {#- What the four measured rows on an issue count (0Z-F, S5).
        Written from `pipeline/synthesis.py` and `pipeline/graph/`, not from the
        row labels — a wrong methodology note is worse than none on a page whose
        whole claim is that we say only what we measure. Each one ends with what
        it does **not** say, which is the distinction this project keeps making
        everywhere else.

        `canon` is the one that would not compress. Whether a cited work counts
        as a foundation or an instrument is decided by looking its OpenAlex
        topic up in two curated lists, and only when the topic is on neither
        does a citation-ratio threshold decide. That is a judgement encoded in a
        file, not a measurement, and the sentence says so rather than pretending
        to a one-line rule. -#}
    <p class="uc-method__p">
      <strong>What the rows on an issue count.</strong>
      <em>tag shift</em> — how many of the day's papers carry a tag, against
      that tag's own daily average over the previous 30 days; listed only above
      3 papers and 3× the average, and nothing at all until the archive holds
      seven days to average over. It compares today with our own archive, so a
      shift can mean our collecting changed rather than the field did.
      <em>canon</em> — works our own archive cites often enough to have been
      resolved and kept, filtered to the ones classified as foundations rather
      than instruments; listed when two of the day's papers cite the same one,
      or when it is the first citation in 90 days and the archive covers that
      long. The foundation-or-instrument split is a curated decision about
      OpenAlex topics, not a measurement, and it is the part of this row to
      treat as ours.
      <em>coupling</em> — pairs of papers citing at least three of the same
      works, today with today and today with a paper from the previous 90 days;
      the shared works are named. Sharing references is not sharing a question,
      and a paper with a long bibliography pairs more easily than a short one.
      <em>institutions</em> — how many of the day's papers carry each name in a
      byline, listed at two, and the same count over 30 days, listed at three.
      A frequency, never a ranking, and it counts affiliations we were given
      rather than work anybody did.
    </p>
    <p class="uc-method__p uc-method__api">
      All of it is also <a href="api.html">available as JSON</a>, with the
      licence and the field meanings written out.
    </p>
  </section>

  {#- The three columns that were here — Method, Honesty, Tags — are gone
      (Launch A, A2). "How this is made" above says all three things and says
      them with the measured numbers attached; keeping both left the page
      making the same three claims twice, forty lines apart, which is the noise
      0Z-C removed from the archive rows for the same reason. The stylesheet
      keeps `.uc-columns`: the design review page is built from these bodies
      and the rule costs nothing. -#}

  <footer class="uc-footer">
    <p class="uc-footer__ilp">Institute for Latent Publics · free, no ads, no metrics</p>
    <p class="uc-footer__build">urban-currents {{ pipeline_version }} · <a href="api.html">API</a></p>
    {# arXiv's terms require this wording exactly, including the disclaimer. #}
    <p class="uc-attribution">
      Thank you to arXiv for use of its open access interoperability. This
      service was not reviewed or approved by, nor does it necessarily express
      or reflect the policies or opinions of, arXiv.
    </p>
  </footer>
</main>
</body>
</html>
